<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fukui Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, with its capital at Fukui. Formed after the abolition of the domains in 1871 and given its present shape in 1881, it covers the old provinces of Echizen and Wakasa. It is known for the Zen temple of Eihei-ji, dinosaur fossils and a concentration of nuclear power stations along its coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukui-prefecture.jpg" alt="Fukui Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 藤谷良秀(Yoshihide Fujitani) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fukui_plain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fukui Prefecture lies in the Chūbu region of Honshū and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishikawa-prefecture">Ishikawa Prefecture</a> to the north, Gifu Prefecture to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a> to the south and Kyoto Prefecture to the southwest. It covers about 4,190 square kilometres and faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, within the historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku region</a>. The western part, formerly Wakasa, is a narrow plain between the mountains and the sea. The larger eastern part, formerly Echizen, has wider plains and holds the capital and most of the population. The area lies within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s snow country, and parts of it are protected as Hakusan National Park and the Echizen-Kaga Kaigan and Wakasa Wan Quasi-National Parks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Kitadani Dinosaur Quarry on the Sugiyama River, in the city of Katsuyama, has yielded dinosaurs such as Fukuiraptor, Fukuisaurus, Koshisaurus, Fukuivenator, Fukuititan and Tambatitanis, and fossils from the prefecture are shown at the Fukui Prefectural Dinosaur Museum.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> originally consisted of the old provinces of Wakasa and Echizen before the prefecture was formed in 1871. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the daimyō of the region bore the surname Matsudaira and descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>. The Matsudaira clan was headquartered at Fukui Castle, which stood on the site of the modern prefectural offices. Fukui was heavily bombed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, and the castle, surrounded by a moat, was demolished; the prefectural government buildings were built on its site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> is the capital and largest city, and other major cities include Sakai, Echizen and Sabae. Sabae produces 90 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s domestically made glasses, and Fukui has long been a centre of papermaking, with makers of traditional Echizen paper. The prefecture is also known for sake, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and soba noodles, and its people speak a distinctive accent, Fukui-ben.</p><p>Among its historic sites are Eihei-ji, a Buddhist training temple founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogen">Dōgen</a> in 1244 on about 33 hectares; the Ichijōdani Asakura Family Historic Ruins; the National Treasures of Myōtsū-ji, its three-storied pagoda and main hall; and Maruoka Castle, built in 1576 and the oldest standing castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Nuclear power stations stand along Wakasa Bay.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fukui Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
